00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.410
High Performance
Computing, for HPC,

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.110
is an aggregation
of computing power

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.390
to solve problems
which are either too

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:25.420
large for standard computers
or would take too long.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.870
It's also referred to as
supercomputing for this reason.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.530
It enables the
simulation or analysis

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.950
of huge volumes of data
that would otherwise not

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.920
be possible with
standard computers.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.250
The common challenge with
high performance computing

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.920
is that you're
producing or processing

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.560
more data than your
infrastructure resources can

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.160
handle, and you have to
wait for weeks or months

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.730
to see the results, which
typically delays the innovation

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.130
and slows down research.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.590
You can think of a high
performance computing system

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:01.110
as a group of computers
called a cluster.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.530
And each computer in a
cluster is called a node.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.200
Each unit in the cluster
has an operating system,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.630
a processor with
multiple cores, storage,

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.630
and networking
capabilities for the units

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.260
to talk to each other.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.000
A smaller cluster,
for example, can

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.050
have 16 nodes with
64 cores, which

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.640
is four cores per
processor, helping you

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.160
solve the problem much faster.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.100
A supercomputer is a much
larger variation of this.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.220
An HPC job that would run
across an on-premises cluster

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:34.350
for three months could
run across 125,000 cores

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:39.180
in 16 hours in the cloud at
little or no incremental cost.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.840
And by augmenting your HPC
environment with Google Cloud,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.060
you gain economies
of scale with access

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.790
to the largest compute
and storage hardware,

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.190
global presence,
robust networking,

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.920
and intelligent automated
management capabilities

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.050
on the cleanest cloud.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.440
Now, how to build HPC
environment on Google Cloud?

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.340
Compute, storage, and networking
are the building blocks

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.140
of high performance computing.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:07.200
Compute Engine provides
customizable virtual machines

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:08.910
running in Google Cloud.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.250
You can scale up
and down as needed

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.080
and choose from a
range of machine

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.940
types for your workloads.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:18.930
We recommend the compute
optimized C2 machines for most

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:21.020
high performance
computing applications,

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:23.490
but you can also select
the general purpose

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:28.770
N1, N2, or N2D for applications
requiring larger memory sizes,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:32.370
or A2 instances if
you need to use GPUs.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.440
For very specific
requirements, you

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.320
can choose custom machine
types with the exact number

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.710
of cores and memory to match
your workload's requirements

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.800
and get the best
performance per dollar.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.830
You could also use cost
effective preemptable VMs,

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.110
which are short-lived
compute instances

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.900
suitable for batch jobs and
fault-tolerant workloads.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.690
Now, the underlying
storage system

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.440
is critical to the performance
of many HPC applications.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.410
You have a lot of storage
options in Google Cloud.

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.350
Cloud storage is a highly
scalable object store

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.180
to store any amount of data.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.840
Persistent disk is durable and
high performance block storage

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.410
for your VM instances.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:16.140
Filestore high scale is a high
performance scale out system

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.670
that makes it easy to mount file
shares on Compute Engine VMs.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.220
Networking, Google's
privately managed

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.800
global network
infrastructure ensures

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:29.160
that your data and applications
are the least exposed

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.740
to the public internet.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.630
[INAUDIBLE] private
cloud VPC networks

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.990
are available to enable
connectivity from your Compute

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.140
Engine VM instances
and configure firewalls

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.820
for your applications.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.070
With placement policies,
you can control

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.190
the placement of your
VMs in our data centers.

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.310
Compact placement policy
provides low latency

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.000
between nodes by placing
instances close together

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.280
within the same
network infrastructure

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.200
to speed communications
between nodes.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.400
Now, let's put it all
together for your HPC workload

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:03.600
on Google Cloud.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.580
First, determine
the compute storage

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.400
and networking requirements
for your HPC code,

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.740
then create your
HPC cluster using

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.720
Compute Engine
instances connected

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.790
to the storage of your choice.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:17.490
Additionally, Google
Cloud supports several job

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.320
schedulers, making
it easy for you

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.810
to start autoscale
virtual machines

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.500
to meet job requirements or spin
down a cluster when a job is

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.350
complete, helping save cost.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.440
Then visualize your
result in BigQuery or AI

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.390
Platform for post-processing.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.670
From there, just
monitor the performance

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.250
and change the
cluster as needed.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.520
Security is important
for any HPC workload.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.370
Google Cloud's secure
by design infrastructure

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.770
protects your
data, applications,

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.290
and users with advanced
antimalware and threat

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:51.600
detection.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.420
High performance computing
drives research, development,

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.430
and innovation in
many industries,

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.680
from rendering the visual
effects of the latest

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.610
blockbuster movie, to
sequencing the human genome

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:05.900
to cure diseases, to risk
analysis in financial services,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:09.560
or even to design the
next generation of cars.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:11.030
If this has piqued
your interest,

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:16.810
visit cloud.google.com/hpc to
learn more and get started.

